:"Country Rock","n":4},{"id":183121849119726,"type":"genre","name":"Cosmic Country","n":1}],"labels":[],"album_labels":[],"own_scenes":[],"scenes":[],"book_tracks":[],"mentions":[],"podcast":[{"kind":"podcast","title":"Popularity in England (1976)","body":"Brimsley Schwartz was mentioned as a prominent band in England during the Ramones' first visit in July 1976, suggesting they were part of the popular pub rock scene. \"Dr. Philgit was the big band at the time in Brimsley Schwartz.\"","year":1976,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"The Ongoing History of New Music","episode":"The History of Alt-Rock: Chapter 4","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=wYuNolSDLFM"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Ambitious Live Performers","body":"The band was ambitious, playing numerous clubs and colleges across England, and they even had the opportunity to open for Paul McCartney. \"Brindsley Schwartz, the band, were ambitious. They played clubs and colleges all over England and even opened for Paul McCartney.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Hit Parade Music History and Music Trivia","episode":"Angry Young Men Edition Part 1","url":"https://shows.acast.com/hit-parade/episodes/695d4f780c30a1408dc2baf3"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Band Naming","body":"The band was named after their guitarist, Brinsley Schwarz. \"Named after their guitarist, Brindsley Schwartz.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Hit Parade Music History and Music Trivia","episode":"Angry Young Men Edition Part 1","url":"https://shows.acast.com/hit-parade/episodes/695d4f780c30a1408dc2baf3"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Initial Rootsy Sound","body":"Initially, their sound was so rootsy that it bordered on American country music. \"This band had a sound so rootsy that at first it bordered on American country music.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Hit Parade Music History and Music Trivia","episode":"Angry Young Men Edition Part 1","url":"https://shows.acast.com/hit-parade/episodes/695d4f780c30a1408dc2baf3"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Members Form The Rumor","body":"After Brinsley Schwarz split, several former members regrouped to form The Rumor, which became Graham Parker's backing band. \"Soon several former members of that band became The Rumor, Graham Parker's backing band.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Hit Parade Music History and Music Trivia","episode":"Angry Young Men Edition Part 1","url":"https://shows.acast.com/hit-parade/episodes/695d4f780c30a1408dc2baf3"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Nick Lowe as \"Secret Weapon\"","body":"The band possessed a \"secret weapon\" in their songwriter, Nick Lowe, who was in his early 20s and hailed from Walton on Thames in Surrey, possessing a notable gift for melody. \"They also had a secret weapon, a songwriter in his early 20s from Walton on Thames in Surrey, who had a gift from Melody. His name was Nick Lo.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Hit Parade Music History and Music Trivia","episode":"Angry Young Men Edition Part 1","url":"https://shows.acast.com/hit-parade/episodes/695d4f780c30a1408dc2baf3"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Prominence in UK Pub Rock Scene","body":"Brinsley Schwarz was a notable band in the English pub rock scene during the Ramones' initial visit to the UK in 1976, mentioned alongside Dr. Feelgood. \"I remember at the time the big thing going on in England was Pobrock, Dr. Felga, was the big band at the time in Brimsley Schwartz.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"The Ongoing History of New Music","episode":"The Original Ramones","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=j8uk7BnbXwU"}]}],"awards":[],"concerts":[],"xref":[],"charts":[{"id":238047137578979,"type":"album","name":"Brinsley Schwarz","list_id":127925552292588,"list":"RYM Top — Rock — Cosmic Country","src":"musthear","rank":38,"year":1970,"note":null,"yt":"rvSF4fESzeA","scaruffi":null},{"id":187337114000796,"type":"album","name":"Despite It All","list_id":109535037444116,"list":